Icarust as used for the PoreLoom closed-loop runs (experiments/20261011_simloop/closed_loop.py).

Base: https://github.com/LooseLab/Icarust commit ef055864183ca2f149b748d29c34fe4ec1e74cac
      (tag v0.0.7a, Cargo.toml version 0.6.0). Applying this patch to it gives the simulator source of every
      closed-loop run in the paper.

    git clone https://github.com/LooseLab/Icarust && cd Icarust
    git checkout ef055864183ca2f149b748d29c34fe4ec1e74cac
    git apply /path/to/PoreLoom/experiments/20261011_simloop/icarust.patch
    cargo build --release --locked        # build requirements as in Icarust's README (protoc, HDF5, OpenSSL)

The paper build used rustc/cargo 1.98.1 and protoc 29.3; cargo test --release --locked runs 33 tests.

Changes:
- Cargo.lock (ignored upstream): dependency versions of the paper build.
- src/e7_audit.rs: writes e7_engineering_audit.jsonl in the run's output directory, one JSON line per event:
  read start (sample, source contig and signal range, length), packet prepared and enqueued,
  unblock or stop_further_data accepted or rejected, read end, and censored read tails (shutdown,
  stream disconnect, capacity stop). analyze_live.py takes ground truth from it.
- src/e7_engine.rs, main.rs, simulation.rs, read_length_distribution.rs, impl_services/data.rs: the profile's
  random_seed is required, and read lengths, ambiguous-base fills, signal noise, per-channel read sampling,
  pore death and recovery, and read IDs are derived from it per named domain (upstream draws from thread_rng
  or fixed seeds), so a seed replays the same sequence of reads on each channel.
- impl_services/data.rs: read-until actions must name the read currently on the channel (stale or duplicate
  actions are rejected and logged); writer and transport retention is bounded (4096 reads, 256 MB) and the run
  stops visibly when it is exceeded; the writer flushes every 1000 reads or 16 MB (upstream 4000 reads).
- main.rs: Ctrl-C requests a graceful shutdown that drains the writer (upstream exits after 10 s); servers bind
  127.0.0.1; the manager reports the configured position port (upstream reports 10001); profiles must use
  400 bp/s.

